n":2025,"team":"KC","distance":0.8696,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Steve Spagnuolo"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Commanders went 8-8-1 in 2022 and were outscored 343 to 321. The defense did most of the work, finishing 4th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 26th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 3rd lowest early-down pass rate and the lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","offense":"The Commanders offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the 3rd highest RPO rate in the league. Carson Wentz ran the passing game, with Terry McLaurin leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Commanders had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 8th or better in all five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was success rate allowed, where it ranked 1st, and its weakest was takeaways per game, where it ranked 29th. Jonathan Allen was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Efe Obada, who had 4 sacks while playing only 37% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the lowest two-high safety rate in the league.","special":"The Commanders were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 83% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 18% of the money on the books.","players":"Carson Wentz played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,755 yards. Terry McLaurin was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 1,191 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Brian Robinson and Antonio Gibson. On defense, Daron Payne led the team with 11.5 sacks and Darrick Forrest picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"This staff is not in the coaching data, so the comparisons below are based on scheme alone. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Steelers and the 2024 Packers under Matt LaFleur. Their defense looked most like the 2024 Texans under DeMeco Ryans and the 2020 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 21st in EPA per play in 2021 to 26th in 2022, a modest decline. The defense moved from 26th in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 4th in 2022, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was 13 personnel usage, which went from 1% to 6%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}